/**
 * What the narrator says for each catalogue event (round 79).
 *
 * Three or four sentences each, about twenty to twenty-five seconds (the
 * owner: "we can make more speech -- 4 or 5 files, make the events more
 * explainable"): what it is, why it happens, and the one fact worth
 * remembering. These are also the text recorded (docs/voice3-text/Part
 * 3L-1.txt to 3L-5.txt) and the entries in narrationScript.js, so the words
 * and the voice agree. Until a recording is in place an event plays
 * without her.
 *
 * Order is recording order. Numbers are written as she says them. Facts
 * follow the event's own card (catalogue/*.js), which gives the sources.
 */
export const CATALOGUE_STORIES = [
  // 1 · Size and scale
  ["scale-planets-between", "Here is a fact that sounds impossible. Line up every other planet, Mercury to Neptune, edge to edge, and together they measure about three hundred and eighty thousand kilometres. That is almost exactly the distance to the Moon. At its average distance they overshoot by a whisker, but when the Moon swings out to its farthest, they all fit, with room to spare."],
  ["scale-thirty-earths", "In most pictures, the Moon looks like it is just next door. It is not. You could line up about thirty Earths, side by side, in the gap between us and the Moon. Light crosses that gap in just over a second. The Apollo astronauts took three days."],
  ["scale-earths-in-sun", "This is the Sun next to the Earth, drawn to scale. Earth is that tiny blue dot, and the Sun is a hundred and nine Earths wide. But fill it up, and it could hold about one point three million Earths. The Sun alone holds almost all the mass of the Solar System."],
  ["scale-great-red-spot", "Jupiter's Great Red Spot is a single storm, wider than the whole Earth. It has been raging for at least two hundred years, with winds faster than four hundred kilometres an hour. But it is shrinking. A hundred and forty years ago, three Earths could have fitted across it. Today, only about one and a third."],
  ["scale-saturn-floats", "Saturn is almost ten Earths wide, and yet it is so light for its size that, in a big enough bathtub, it would float. It is made mostly of hydrogen and helium, the lightest things there are, so its average density is less than water's. Earth, made of rock and iron, would sink straight to the bottom."],
  ["scale-venus-day-year", "On Venus, a day is longer than a year. It takes two hundred and twenty-five Earth days to go once around the Sun, but two hundred and forty-three to turn once on its axis. And it spins backwards, so there the Sun rises in the west. From one sunrise to the next takes about a hundred and seventeen Earth days."],
  ["scale-olympus-everest", "This is Everest, almost nine kilometres high, the tallest mountain on Earth. And this is Olympus Mons, on Mars: about twenty-two kilometres high, two and a half Everests, and as wide as France. Mars has no moving plates, so its volcanoes sat over the same hot spot for ages, and just kept growing."],
  ["scale-valles-marineris", "Mars has a canyon that would stretch right across the United States. Valles Marineris is about four thousand kilometres long, and in places seven kilometres deep. Our Grand Canyon would fit inside one of its side branches. It was not carved by a river. As Mars's giant volcanoes swelled up beside it, the ground split open."],
  ["scale-moon-receding", "The Moon is slowly drifting away from us, about four centimetres every year, roughly as fast as your fingernails grow. We know, because astronauts left mirrors on the Moon, and we bounce lasers off them. The tides the Moon raises on Earth pull it gently outward, and slow our spin. Long ago, a day on Earth lasted only about eighteen hours."],
  ["scale-light-travel", "When you look at the Sun, you are seeing the past. Its light takes eight minutes and twenty seconds to reach us. To reach Jupiter, it takes forty-three minutes, and to reach Neptune, more than four hours. Watch one flash leave the Sun, and race outward past every planet."],
  ["scale-photon-escape", "The light on your face left the Sun eight minutes ago. But that is only the end of its journey. It was born deep in the core, then spent tens of thousands of years bouncing its way out, absorbed and thrown off again in random directions, over and over. Some of today's sunlight began that trip before the first farms on Earth."],
  ["scale-jupiter-balance", "Jupiter is the heavyweight. Put it on one side of a cosmic balance, and every other planet on the other side. Jupiter still wins. It is two and a half times heavier than all of them combined, because it formed first, and grabbed most of the gas left over from the birth of the Sun."],
  // 2 · The Sun
  ["sun-flare", "A solar flare is a sudden flash of light on the Sun. Twisted magnetic fields above a group of sunspots snap and reconnect, releasing in minutes as much energy as billions of nuclear bombs. Its light reaches Earth in eight minutes, before anything else, and the biggest flares can black out radio on the day side of the planet."],
  ["sun-carrington", "In September eighteen fifty-nine, the biggest solar storm on record struck Earth. The night before, the astronomer Richard Carrington had watched a brilliant flare on the Sun. Less than a day later, auroras lit up skies near the tropics, bright enough to read by, and telegraph wires sparked and carried messages with their batteries unplugged."],
  ["sun-gannon-storm", "In May twenty twenty-four, a burst of eruptions from one huge group of sunspots hit Earth one after another. It was the strongest geomagnetic storm in more than twenty years. Auroras were photographed as far south as India, Mexico and the Canary Islands, and millions of people saw the northern lights for the first time in their lives."],
  ["sun-cycle", "The Sun has a heartbeat about eleven years long. At solar minimum it is calm and almost spotless. At solar maximum it is covered in sunspots, flares and eruptions. And at every maximum, its magnetic north and south poles swap places. The cycle that began in twenty nineteen peaked around the end of twenty twenty-four."],
  ["sun-prominence", "A prominence is a giant loop of glowing gas, many times taller than the Earth, held up above the Sun's surface by its magnetic field. Some hang quietly for weeks. Others become unstable and erupt, flinging billions of tonnes of plasma out into space."],
  ["sun-coronal-hole", "Coronal holes are dark patches in the Sun's outer atmosphere. Here the magnetic field does not loop back down, but opens straight out into space, so hot gas escapes freely, as the fastest solar wind, at around eight hundred kilometres a second. When one faces Earth, auroras often follow a few days later."],
  ["sun-differential-rotation", "The Sun is not solid, so it does not turn as one piece. Its equator spins once in about twenty-five days, but near the poles a turn takes about thirty-five. This uneven spin winds up the Sun's magnetic field like a spring, and that is what drives the sunspots, the flares and the whole eleven-year cycle."],
  ["sun-granulation", "Up close, the Sun's surface is boiling. Each bright cell is a column of hot gas the size of a country, rising from below, cooling, and sinking again at its dark edges. A single cell lasts only about ten minutes, and there are around four million of them on the Sun at any moment."],
  ["sun-neutrinos", "The Sun's core makes more than light. It also fires out neutrinos, ghostly particles that barely touch anything. Every second, tens of billions of them pass through your thumbnail, and straight through the whole Earth, day and night. Catching even a few of them takes a giant tank deep underground."],
  ["sun-red-giant", "The Sun will not last forever. In about five billion years, it will run short of hydrogen in its core and swell into a red giant, swallowing Mercury and Venus, and maybe Earth. Then it will puff off its outer layers as a glowing cloud, and shrink into a white dwarf, about the size of the Earth."],
  // 3 · Earth and the Moon
  ["lunar-eclipse", "In a total lunar eclipse, the Moon slides into Earth's shadow. But it does not disappear. It turns a deep coppery red, because Earth's atmosphere bends a little sunlight into the shadow. You are seeing the light of every sunrise and sunset on Earth, all at once, falling on the Moon."],
  ["annular-eclipse", "The Moon's orbit is an oval, so sometimes it is a little farther from us, and looks a little smaller. If an eclipse happens then, the Moon cannot cover the whole Sun. It leaves a blazing ring of fire around its edge. That is an annular eclipse, from the Latin word for a ring."],
  ["eclipse-2027", "On the second of August twenty twenty-seven, the Moon's shadow will sweep across North Africa and the Middle East. Over Luxor, in Egypt, the Sun will be completely hidden for more than six minutes, one of the longest total eclipses of the century. The Moon will be near its closest to Earth, and Earth near its farthest from the Sun."],
  ["supermoon", "The Moon's orbit is not a perfect circle but an oval, so its distance from us changes every month. At its farthest point, called apogee, it looks a little smaller. At its closest, called perigee, it looks a little bigger. A full Moon near perigee is called a supermoon. It is about fourteen percent wider and thirty percent brighter than a full Moon at apogee. Side by side the difference is clear, even though by eye it is hard to tell."],
  ["moon-phases", "The Moon is always half lit by the Sun. What changes is how much of that lit half faces us. As the Moon circles Earth each month, we see a thin crescent, then a half, then a full Moon, and back again. The phases are just the Moon's day and night, seen from here."],
  ["tides", "The Moon and the Sun both pull on Earth's oceans, raising two bulges of water on opposite sides of the planet. As Earth turns through them, the tide comes in and goes out about twice a day. When the Sun and Moon line up, we get the highest tides of all, spring tides. When they pull at right angles, the tides are gentle, neap tides."],
  ["tidal-locking", "The Moon always shows us the same face. That is not a coincidence. Long ago, Earth's pull on the Moon slowed its spin, until it turned exactly once for every trip around us. No one saw the far side until a spacecraft photographed it in nineteen fifty-nine. Most of the big moons in the Solar System are locked the same way."],
  ["libration", "The Moon does not hold perfectly still. Its oval orbit, and its slight tilt, make it seem to rock from side to side and nod up and down over a month. Thanks to that wobble, we can see a little way around its edges, about fifty-nine percent of its surface in all, not just half."],
  ["earthshine", "Look at a thin crescent Moon on a clear evening, and you can often see the rest of it, glowing faintly. That glow is earthshine. It is sunlight that has bounced off the Earth, travelled to the Moon, and bounced back to your eyes. Leonardo da Vinci was the first to explain it, five hundred years ago."],
  ["theia-impact", "Our Moon was probably born in a collision. About four and a half billion years ago, a world about the size of Mars, often called Theia, smashed into the young Earth. The impact threw a ring of molten rock into orbit, and within a short time, it gathered itself into the Moon."],
  ["moonquakes", "The Moon still quakes. Instruments left by the Apollo astronauts recorded thousands of moonquakes. Most are tiny, but shallow ones can reach magnitude five. And because the Moon is so dry and cold, it does not damp the shaking. The whole Moon can ring like a bell for many minutes."],
  ["chicxulub", "Sixty-six million years ago, an asteroid about ten kilometres wide struck what is now the coast of Mexico. It blasted a crater a hundred and eighty kilometres across, and threw so much dust and soot into the sky that the Sun was dimmed for years. Three quarters of all species died out, including every dinosaur that could not fly."],
  ["tunguska", "On the thirtieth of June nineteen-oh-eight, a space rock exploded in the air above Siberia. The blast flattened about eighty million trees, across more than two thousand square kilometres of forest. But it left no crater, because it burst a few kilometres up, before it ever reached the ground."],
  ["chelyabinsk", "On the fifteenth of February twenty thirteen, a rock about twenty metres wide streaked across the morning sky over Russia, briefly brighter than the Sun. It burst about thirty kilometres up. The shock wave arrived minutes later, shattering windows across the city of Chelyabinsk. No telescope had seen it coming."],
  ["daylight-fireball-1972", "On the tenth of August nineteen seventy-two, a space rock several metres across skimmed through Earth's upper atmosphere over the western United States and Canada. Hundreds of people saw it blaze across the daytime sky. Then, like a stone skipping on water, it bounced back out into space, and carried on around the Sun."],
  ["apophis-2029", "On Friday the thirteenth of April twenty twenty-nine, the asteroid Apophis, about three hundred and forty metres wide, will pass about thirty-two thousand kilometres from Earth's surface. That is closer than some satellites. It will be bright enough to see without a telescope, and we are now sure it will miss."],
  ["mini-moon-2024", "In the autumn of twenty twenty-four, Earth briefly had a second moon. A small asteroid, only about ten metres across, drifted slowly enough to be caught by Earth's gravity in a loose loop. It stayed for about two months, then drifted away again, back into orbit around the Sun."],
  ["leonid-storm-1833", "Every thirty-three years, Comet Tempel–Tuttle swings past the Sun, and the Sun's heat drives dust off it. That dust spreads out along the comet's path, like a river of tiny grains. Earth's orbit crosses that river, and every November we pass through it. The comet travels the opposite way round the Sun to us, so the grains hit our air head-on, at seventy-one kilometres a second, and burn up as shooting stars. On the night of the twelfth of November eighteen thirty-three, Earth hit a dense, young stretch of the trail. Over North America, tens of thousands of shooting stars an hour fell like snow, all streaming from one point in the constellation Leo. The study of meteors as a science began that night."],
  ["precession", "Earth spins around an axis, an imaginary line through the North and South Poles. Today the north end of that line points almost exactly at one star: Polaris, the North Star. So as Earth turns, the whole night sky seems to wheel around Polaris, while Polaris alone stays still. That is why travellers have used it for centuries to find north. But the axis itself slowly wobbles, like a spinning top, tracing a circle once every twenty-six thousand years. When the first pyramids were built, it pointed near a faint star called Thuban. In about twelve thousand years it will point near Vega, one of the brightest stars in the sky, and Vega will be our pole star."],
  ["magnetic-reversal", "Earth's magnetic field is made by molten iron churning in the core, and every so often it flips. North becomes south. We can read the record in old lava flows, which froze with the field of their time locked inside. The last full reversal was about seven hundred and eighty thousand years ago."],
  ["steve", "STEVE is a narrow ribbon of purple light that sometimes appears in the sky, below the aurora. It was named by aurora watchers, and later given a scientific name to fit. It is not an aurora at all. It glows from a river of very hot gas, rushing westward high above the ground."],
  ["zodiacal-light", "On a dark, moonless spring evening, once twilight has ended, look west, where the Sun went down. A faint cone of white light rises from the horizon, leaning along the path that the Sun, the Moon and the planets follow across the sky. This is zodiacal light. It does not glow by itself. It is sunlight, scattered by countless grains of dust drifting between the planets, much of it shed by comets. It is widest and brightest near the horizon, where the dust is closest to the Sun. In autumn, look east before dawn instead. There it was once called the false dawn."],
  // 4 · Mercury, Venus and Mars
  ["transit-mercury", "Now and then, Mercury passes directly between the Earth and the Sun. Through a safe solar telescope, it looks like a tiny black dot, crossing the Sun's face over a few hours. It happens about thirteen times a century. The last was in November twenty nineteen, and the next is in November twenty thirty-two."],
  ["transit-venus", "In June twenty twelve, Venus crossed the face of the Sun, a round black silhouette. Transits of Venus come in pairs, eight years apart, more than a century apart. In the past, astronomers sailed around the world to time them, and used them to measure the size of the Solar System. The next will not happen until December twenty-one seventeen."],
  ["mercury-double-sunrise", "On parts of Mercury, the Sun rises, stops, slips back below the horizon, and then rises again for good. Mercury spins slowly, and when it swings closest to the Sun, its orbit briefly runs faster than its spin, so the Sun seems to go backwards across the sky."],
  ["venus-superrotation", "Venus turns once every two hundred and forty-three days. But the top of its clouds races around the whole planet in just four days, sixty times faster than the ground below. Planetary scientists call it super-rotation, and exactly what drives it is still being worked out."],
  ["venus-volcano", "Venus is still volcanically alive. In twenty twenty-three, scientists looked again at radar images taken in nineteen ninety-one, eight months apart. They found a volcanic vent that had grown and changed shape in between, and filled with fresh lava. It was the first direct sign of a recent eruption on Venus."],
  ["mars-opposition", "About every two years and two months, Earth catches up with Mars on its faster inside orbit, and passes it. Mars then sits opposite the Sun in our sky, rising at sunset and shining all night at its brightest. It is the best time to see it, and the best time to launch spacecraft to it."],
  ["mars-retrograde", "Every two years or so, Mars seems to stop, move backwards across the stars for a few weeks, and then turn forward again. For thousands of years this loop puzzled sky watchers. It is only an illusion: Earth, on the faster inside track, is overtaking Mars, just as a car you pass on a motorway seems to slide backwards."],
  ["mars-blue-sunset", "On Mars, sunsets are blue. The fine red dust in its air scatters red light across the whole sky by day, but near the setting Sun, the dust scatters blue light forward, toward your eyes. So the glow around the Sun at dusk turns a cool, pale blue."],
  ["mars-dust-devils", "Whirlwinds sweep across Mars, much bigger than any on Earth, some of them several kilometres tall. As they wander across the plains, they lift away the bright surface dust and leave dark, looping tracks behind them, like scribbles drawn across the planet."],
  ["mars-spiders", "Every spring at Mars's south pole, the Sun shines through a layer of clear carbon dioxide ice and warms the ground underneath. The ice turns to gas from below, pressure builds, and it bursts out in jets of gas and dust. Year after year, they carve channels that look like giant spiders."],
  ["mars-avalanches", "In spring on Mars, near the north pole, the Sun warms cliffs of ice and dust half a kilometre tall. Frost cracks and breaks away, and avalanches crash down the slopes, throwing clouds of dust into the air. Spacecraft orbiting Mars have caught them in the act."],
  ["phobos-fall", "Mars's little moon Phobos orbits so close that it goes round faster than Mars spins, and the tides it raises are pulling it slowly inward, about two metres every hundred years. In thirty to fifty million years, Mars's gravity will tear it apart, and spread it into a ring around the planet."],
  // 5 · Asteroids
  ["asteroid-collision", "In January twenty-ten, astronomers found what looked like a comet in the asteroid belt, with a strange X-shaped head and a long dusty tail. It was not a comet. It was the wreckage of two asteroids that had smashed into each other, about a year before, at around five kilometres a second."],
  ["asteroid-spin-apart", "Sunlight can push on a small asteroid. Warmed on one side, it gives off heat unevenly, and over millions of years that tiny push can spin it faster and faster, until it can no longer hold together. In twenty thirteen, the Hubble telescope watched one asteroid break apart into ten pieces."],
  ["vesta-rheasilvia", "About a billion years ago, the asteroid Vesta was struck so hard that the impact gouged out a crater five hundred kilometres wide, almost as wide as Vesta itself. Its central mountain is more than twice as tall as Everest. Pieces blasted off Vesta still land on Earth today as meteorites."],
  ["ceres-bright-spots", "Ceres, the largest object in the asteroid belt, has bright spots that shine out of the dark crater Occator. They are salt. Salty water seeped up from below the surface, the water boiled away into space, and the salt was left behind, gleaming in the sunlight."],
  ["phaethon", "Phaethon is a rock that swoops in closer to the Sun than Mercury ever goes, baked to more than seven hundred degrees. The heat cracks it and sheds dust along its orbit. And every December, Earth passes through that dust, and we see the Geminid meteor shower, one of the best of the year."],
  ["bennu-particles", "When a spacecraft arrived at the asteroid Bennu, it saw something no one expected: Bennu was throwing pebbles into space. Small bits of rock pop off its surface every few days. Some escape, some go into orbit for a while, and some fall back down onto the asteroid."],
  // 6 · Jupiter
  ["jupiter-grs-shrinking", "Jupiter's Great Red Spot has been watched for nearly two hundred years, and it is getting smaller. In the eighteen-eighties, it was about forty thousand kilometres across, three Earths wide. Today it is about sixteen thousand, and it has become rounder. No one knows if it will one day vanish."],
  ["jupiter-belt-fade", "In twenty-ten, astronomers pointed their telescopes at Jupiter and found one of its two great dark belts was simply gone. The South Equatorial Belt had been hidden by a layer of bright, high clouds. Months later, a dark outbreak began, and the belt came back. It has faded and returned like this several times."],
  ["io-flux-tube", "Io and Jupiter are wired together. As Io moves through Jupiter's magnetic field, it acts like a giant generator, driving an electric current of millions of amps along the field lines between them. Where that current reaches Jupiter, it makes a glowing spot in the aurora, Io's own footprint."],
  ["laplace-resonance", "Three of Jupiter's big moons move in step. For every one orbit Ganymede makes, Europa makes two, and Io makes four. Their regular tugs on each other keep their orbits stretched, which keeps Io's volcanoes and Europa's ocean heated. And all three can never line up on the same side of Jupiter."],
  ["jupiter-shadow-transit", "When one of Jupiter's big moons passes between Jupiter and the Sun, its shadow falls on the cloud tops as a small, perfectly black dot. Anyone standing under it on Jupiter would see a total eclipse. Sometimes two, or even three, moon shadows cross Jupiter at the same time."],
  ["europa-tides", "Europa is covered in ice, but underneath lies an ocean of salty water, with perhaps twice as much water as all of Earth's oceans. Jupiter's gravity squeezes and stretches Europa as it orbits, and that flexing warms the inside, keeping the ocean from freezing. It is one of the best places to look for life beyond Earth."],
  ["jupiter-lightning", "Jupiter has lightning, deep down in its water clouds, flashing in giant thunderstorms. The Juno spacecraft found it is most common near the poles. High above the storms, there are also brief red flashes called sprites, the same kind of upper-air lightning seen above thunderstorms on Earth."],
  // 7 · Saturn
  ["saturn-hexagon", "At Saturn's north pole there is a jet stream shaped like a hexagon, with six straight sides, each one wider than the Earth. It has been there at least since the Voyager probes flew past in the early nineteen-eighties. It is thought to form where winds of different speeds meet, in a stable, wavy pattern."],
  ["ring-plane-crossing", "Saturn's rings are hundreds of thousands of kilometres wide, but in most places only about ten metres thick. So about every fifteen years, when Earth lines up with the plane of the rings, we see them edge-on, and they almost disappear. It last happened in March twenty twenty-five."],
  ["ring-rain", "Saturn's rings are slowly raining into the planet. Tiny ice grains become electrically charged and are pulled along the magnetic field, down into Saturn's atmosphere. The Cassini spacecraft measured this ring rain. At this rate, the rings may be mostly gone in a few hundred million years, so we may be living at the best time to see them."],
  ["ring-propellers", "Hidden in Saturn's rings are tiny moonlets, too small to see, only a few hundred metres across. As they orbit, their gravity stirs up the ring particles on either side, carving gaps shaped like aeroplane propellers. The Cassini spacecraft found many of them."],
  ["shepherd-moons", "Saturn's thin F ring is guarded by two small moons, Prometheus just inside it and Pandora just outside. Their gravity herds the ring's particles and keeps the ring narrow. And every time Prometheus swings close, it pulls out streamers of ice, twisting the ring into knots and braids."],
  ["titan-methane-rain", "Titan, Saturn's largest moon, has weather. It is far too cold for water to flow, but methane, which is a gas on Earth, falls there as rain. It carves rivers, and fills lakes and seas near the poles, under a thick orange haze. Titan is the only place besides Earth with liquid on its surface."],
  ["hyperion-tumble", "Saturn's moon Hyperion looks like a giant sponge, full of deep craters, because it is mostly empty space inside. And it does not spin in any regular way. Tugged by Saturn's big moon Titan, it tumbles chaotically, so its spin can never be predicted for long."],
  ["iapetus-two-faces", "Iapetus is a moon with two faces. The side that leads as it orbits is as dark as coal. The other side is as bright as snow. Dark dust swept up from farther out darkens the leading side, which then warms in the sunlight, and its ice moves away to the bright side, making the difference even starker."],
  // 8 · Uranus, Neptune and beyond
  ["uranus-tipped", "Uranus spins on its side. Its axis is tipped over by ninety-eight degrees, so it rolls around the Sun like a ball. The most likely explanation is a giant collision long ago, with a world several times the size of Earth, that knocked the young planet over."],
  ["uranus-seasons", "Because Uranus lies on its side, its seasons are extreme. During each eighty-four-year orbit, one pole points almost straight at the Sun for decades, while the other is in darkness. So each pole gets about forty-two years of sunlight, then forty-two years of night."],
  ["neptune-dark-spot", "In nineteen eighty-nine, the Voyager Two spacecraft found a dark storm on Neptune as big as the Earth, which became known as the Great Dark Spot. When the Hubble Space Telescope looked in nineteen ninety-four, it was gone. Neptune's storms come and go within a few years."],
  ["neptune-winds", "Neptune has the fastest winds in the Solar System, about two thousand kilometres an hour, faster than the speed of sound on Earth. And it is the planet farthest from the Sun, getting the least sunlight of all. Where the energy for such winds comes from is still a puzzle."],
  ["diamond-rain", "Deep inside Uranus and Neptune, the pressure is millions of times greater than on Earth. There, carbon from methane may be squeezed into diamonds, which slowly sink toward the core, like hail. Experiments with powerful lasers on Earth have made tiny diamonds in the same way."],
  ["triton-doom", "Triton, Neptune's largest moon, orbits backwards, against Neptune's spin. That means it was probably captured from the Kuiper Belt, long ago. Neptune's tides are slowly drawing it inward, and in a few billion years, it will be torn apart, and may become a ring."],
  ["pluto-charon-dance", "Pluto and its moon Charon are so close in size that they are almost a double world. They are locked face to face, each always showing the other the same side. And they both circle a point in the empty space between them, so Pluto wobbles as Charon goes round."],
  ["charon-red-cap", "Charon, Pluto's largest moon, has a red cap on its north pole. Gas slowly escapes from Pluto's atmosphere, and some of it is caught by Charon. In the long polar winter it freezes onto the pole, and when sunlight returns, it slowly turns the ice reddish brown."],
  ["arrokoth-merger", "Arrokoth, far beyond Pluto, is made of two small worlds stuck together, like a cosmic snowman. Billions of years ago, they circled each other, slowly drew together, and touched at walking pace, so gently that neither was broken. It shows how the very first building blocks of planets came together."],
  ["gliese-710", "In about one point three million years, a star called Gliese seven-ten will pass through the outer edge of our Solar System, about ten thousand times farther away than the Sun is from Earth. Its gravity will disturb the Oort Cloud, and send a shower of comets drifting inward over the following million years."],
  // 9 · Comets
  ["halley-2061", "Halley's Comet comes back about every seventy-six years. It was seen by ancient astronomers, and was woven into the Bayeux Tapestry in ten sixty-six. Edmond Halley predicted its return, and it came back, as he said, after his death. It will next be at its closest to the Sun in the summer of twenty sixty-one."],
  ["hale-bopp", "In nineteen ninety-seven, Comet Hale–Bopp hung in our skies for about a year and a half, visible even from bright cities. Its icy core was about sixty kilometres wide, many times larger than most comets. It had two tails, a straight blue tail of gas and a broad white tail of dust."],
  ["tsuchinshan-atlas", "In October twenty twenty-four, Comet Tsuchinshan–ATLAS lit up evening skies around the world. For a few days it showed something rare: a thin spike pointing toward the Sun, an anti-tail. It was not really pointing sunward. We were seeing its dust trail from the side, lined up with Earth."],
  ["neowise", "In July twenty twenty, Comet NEOWISE swung past the Sun and became the brightest comet seen from the northern hemisphere in more than twenty years, with a long curving tail. It will not be back for about seven thousand years."],
  ["holmes-outburst", "In October twenty-oh-seven, Comet Holmes suddenly brightened about half a million times in less than a day, becoming visible without a telescope. Its expanding cloud of dust kept growing, until for a while, it was bigger than the Sun."],
  ["comet-breakup", "Comets can fall apart. Comet Schwassmann–Wachmann Three began breaking up in nineteen ninety-five. By two thousand and six, it had split into dozens of pieces, strung out along its orbit, each one with a tail of its own."],
  ["encke-tail", "In April twenty-oh-seven, a spacecraft was watching Comet Encke when a blast of plasma from the Sun swept over it. The comet's ion tail was snapped clean off, and drifted away into space. The comet soon grew a new one."],
  ["comet-two-tails", "A comet has two tails. One is made of dust, pushed gently by sunlight, so it curves along the comet's path and shines white. The other is made of gas, blown straight outward by the solar wind, glowing blue. Both always point away from the Sun, whichever way the comet is moving."],
  // 10 · Alignments
  ["great-conjunction", "Every twenty years, Jupiter and Saturn appear to pass each other in our sky. In December twenty twenty they came within a tenth of a degree, closer than they had looked since sixteen twenty-three. Through a small telescope, both planets and their moons fitted in the same view."],
  ["planet-parade", "Sometimes several planets gather along one arc of the evening sky, and people call it a planet parade. But they are not lined up in space. All the planets orbit in nearly the same flat plane, so from Earth we always see them along the same path across the sky."],
  ["lunar-occultation", "Now and then, the Moon slides in front of a planet and hides it. Because the Moon has no air, the planet does not fade. It vanishes at the Moon's edge in a second or two. When Saturn is hidden, you can watch its rings disappear, and then reappear on the other side."],
  // 11 · Beyond the Solar System
  ["betelgeuse-dimming", "Betelgeuse, the red star at Orion's shoulder, is one of the biggest stars we can see, about five hundred and fifty light-years away. Put where the Sun is, its surface would reach past the orbit of Mars, toward Jupiter. In the winter of twenty nineteen and twenty twenty, it faded to about a third of its usual light, and people wondered if it was about to explode. It was not. In twenty nineteen, its boiling surface threw off a huge bubble of gas from its southern half. As the gas moved away, it cooled, and condensed into dust, which blocked part of the star from our view. By April it had recovered. Betelgeuse will explode one day, probably in about a hundred thousand years. From Earth, it will shine as brightly as a half Moon for weeks, visible in daylight. But it is far enough away that it cannot harm us. A supernova would have to be within about fifty light-years to damage Earth's ozone layer."],
  ["t-crb-nova", "Beside the Northern Crown, between the bright stars Arcturus and Vega, is a star too faint to see without a telescope. It is T Coronae Borealis, about three thousand light-years away. In May eighteen sixty-six, it suddenly became as bright as the North Star. In February nineteen forty-six, eighty years later, it did it again. It is a recurrent nova: a white dwarf, circling a red giant every two hundred and twenty-eight days, collecting its gas, and every eighty years or so, exploding. Before nineteen forty-six it dimmed a little, then brightened. Since twenty fifteen, it has been doing the same again, so astronomers think the next eruption is due any time. When it comes, it will last only a few days. Look beside the Northern Crown."],
  ["grb-boat", "On the ninth of October twenty twenty-two, the brightest burst of gamma rays ever recorded hit Earth. Astronomers called it the BOAT: the brightest of all time. It came from a galaxy about two point four billion light-years away, in the direction of Sagitta. A massive, fast-spinning star had run out of fuel and collapsed straight into a black hole. Gas swirling into it launched two jets at almost the speed of light, which drilled out through the star, and one of them was pointed at Earth. Even after two billion years of travel, its gamma rays disturbed Earth's upper atmosphere, and overwhelmed many of the instruments that caught them. A burst as bright as this one may come along only once in ten thousand years."],
  ["sn-1987a", "On the twenty-third of February nineteen eighty-seven, deep underground in Japan, the United States and Russia, detectors caught about two dozen neutrinos in thirteen seconds. Hours later, a new star appeared in the Large Magellanic Cloud, a small galaxy beside our own, a hundred and sixty-eight thousand light-years away. Supernova nineteen eighty-seven A was the nearest seen in almost four hundred years, and the first whose neutrinos were caught. Its star, a blue supergiant called Sanduleak minus sixty-nine two hundred and two, about twenty times the mass of the Sun, had collapsed. The neutrinos escaped at once; the light followed hours later. Around it are three rings of gas the star shed twenty thousand years before. Since two thousand and one, the debris has been crashing into the inner ring, lighting it up like a string of pearls. In twenty twenty-four, the Webb telescope found signs of the neutron star at its heart."],
  ["crab-supernova", "On the fourth of July ten fifty-four, astronomers in China recorded a guest star in Taurus, near the star Zeta Tauri. It was so bright that it could be seen in daylight for twenty-three days, and at night for almost two years. Six and a half thousand light-years away, a star about ten times the mass of the Sun had run out of fuel. Its core collapsed, in less than a second, into a neutron star, and the rest of the star was blasted into space. Nearly a thousand years later, that debris is still flying outward at about fifteen hundred kilometres a second. It is the Crab Nebula, about eleven light-years across, its filaments glowing in red and orange, its centre a blue haze lit by the neutron star, which still spins thirty times every second. This is the Crab Nebula today."],
  ["gw150914", "On the fourteenth of September twenty fifteen, both LIGO detectors, three thousand kilometres apart, felt the same tiny shiver in space, a stretch smaller than a thousandth of the width of a proton. A billion and three hundred million years earlier, two black holes, thirty-six and twenty-nine times the mass of the Sun, had been circling each other. Black holes bend light: the stars behind them appear smeared into rings. As they circled, they stirred space itself, sending out gravitational waves, and lost energy, and drew closer. In the last fifth of a second they whirled around dozens of times, and merged into one black hole of sixty-two Suns. The missing three Suns' worth of mass went out as gravitational waves. It was the first time anyone had felt them, a century after Einstein predicted them."],
  ["magnetar-flare", "On the twenty-seventh of December twenty-oh-four, a flash of gamma rays swamped detectors on spacecraft all over the Solar System. It came from SGR eighteen oh six minus twenty, a magnetar on the far side of our galaxy, tens of thousands of light-years away. A magnetar is a neutron star, about twenty kilometres across, with the strongest magnetic field known, a thousand trillion times Earth's. That field strains the star's solid crust until it cracks: a starquake. The field snaps into a new shape, and in a fifth of a second the magnetar released more energy than the Sun gives out in a hundred thousand years. A cloud of fireball stayed trapped in its field, pulsing every seven and a half seconds as the star turned. Even from so far away, its gamma rays disturbed Earth's upper atmosphere."],
  ["tidal-disruption", "In September twenty nineteen, a sky survey saw the centre of a galaxy about two hundred and fifteen million light-years away suddenly brighten. A star like the Sun had strayed too close to the black hole at its heart, a black hole of about a million Suns. Near a black hole like that, the pull on a star's near side is much stronger than on its far side. Close enough, that difference beats the star's own gravity. The star was pulled into a long stream of gas: astronomers say spaghettified. About half of the gas flew off into space. The other half fell back, swirled into a hot disc, and blazed for months. This event, AT twenty nineteen q i z, is one of the best watched of these tidal disruptions."],
  ["sgr-a-flares", "Toward the constellation of Sagittarius, behind thick clouds of dust, lies the centre of our galaxy, twenty-six thousand seven hundred light-years away. Rise above the Milky Way, and dive into its heart. There, stars race round something unseen. One, called S two, swings round it every sixteen years, at up to seven thousand six hundred and fifty kilometres a second. To pull a star like that, the unseen thing must weigh four point three million Suns, packed into a space smaller than our Solar System. It is a black hole: Sagittarius A star. In twenty twenty-two, the Event Horizon Telescope photographed its shadow, ringed by glowing gas. It is starved, swallowing only a trickle, but about once a day, a clump of hot gas circling close to the edge flares up, in under an hour. For proving it was there, Andrea Ghez and Reinhard Genzel shared the twenty twenty Nobel Prize in Physics."],
  ["fast-radio-bursts", "Radio telescopes sometimes catch flashes that last just a thousandth of a second, and give out as much energy as the Sun does in days. These fast radio bursts arrive about a thousand times a day, from all over the sky, almost all from other galaxies, billions of light-years away. What makes them? On the twenty-eighth of April twenty twenty, one came from inside our own galaxy, from a magnetar called SGR nineteen thirty-five plus twenty-one fifty-four, about thirty thousand light-years away. Its magnetic field strained and snapped, and with a burst of X-rays came a millisecond flash of radio waves. On the way to Earth, the thin gas between the stars slows the lower radio frequencies, so a burst arrives as a sweep, high notes first. How long the sweep is tells how far the burst has travelled."],
  ["proxima-superflare", "Our nearest star after the Sun is Proxima Centauri, four point two four light-years away, a small, cool red dwarf, a seventh the width of the Sun. Around it orbits a rocky planet, Proxima b, twenty times closer to its star than Earth is to the Sun. On the eighteenth of March twenty sixteen, Proxima flared. A tangle of its magnetic field snapped, and in minutes the star became sixty-eight times brighter, bright enough, for a moment, to see with the naked eye. Red dwarfs like Proxima are churned right through by convection, which tangles their fields, so their flares are far fiercer than our Sun's for their size. Our Sun's biggest flares brighten it by less than one percent. A blast like Proxima's would scour its planet, and flares like it, several times a year, could strip away any ozone layer it might have."],
  ["milky-way-andromeda", "On a dark night, in the constellation of Andromeda, you can see a faint smudge. It is the Andromeda Galaxy, a trillion stars, two and a half million light-years away: the most distant thing you can see with the naked eye. And it is coming toward us, at about a hundred and ten kilometres a second. For years, astronomers expected a head-on collision in about four and a half billion years. Now, with better measurements and the pull of the neighbouring galaxies included, it looks like a coin toss: about a fifty percent chance that the two merge within ten billion years. If they do, their stars will almost never collide; they are too far apart. Instead, the two spirals will swing through each other, fling out long tails of stars, and settle into one great elliptical galaxy."],
  // 12 · Deep sky, round 88: the new voyages (recorded round 89: speech-forDeep-sky-1 to -4)
  ["what-is-a-nova", "What is a nova? The word is Latin for new. For thousands of years, people now and then saw a new star appear where there had been none, blaze for a while, and fade. We now know that most of these are not new stars at all, but old ones, flaring up. And there are very different ways for a star to flare. A true nova happens on a white dwarf, the small, dense, Earth-sized core left behind when a star like the Sun dies. If a white dwarf has a partner star close by, its gravity can pull gas off that partner. The gas piles up on the white dwarf's surface, squeezed and heated, until it ignites all at once, like a hydrogen bomb the size of a planet. The layer is blown off into space, the star shines thousands of times brighter for a few weeks, and then it settles down. The white dwarf survives, and starts collecting again. A supernova is something far bigger. Super means it destroys the whole star. Either a white dwarf grows too heavy and blows itself completely apart, or a giant star many times the Sun's mass runs out of fuel, and its core collapses in less than a second. For a few weeks, one supernova can outshine an entire galaxy of a hundred billion stars. And a kilonova is the flash when two neutron stars, the crushed cores of dead giant stars, crash into each other. It is about a thousand times brighter than a nova, which is where its name comes from, but much fainter than a supernova. Its debris forges gold, platinum and uranium. So, a nova is a flare on a surviving star. A supernova is the death of a star. And a kilonova is the collision of two dead ones. Let us travel to each of them."],
  ["classical-nova", "In August nineteen seventy-five, a new star appeared in the constellation of Cygnus, the Swan. One night there was nothing there that any ordinary telescope could see. By the next evening it was one of the brightest stars in the sky. Its name is V fifteen hundred Cygni, and it lies about five thousand one hundred light-years away. There, a white dwarf circles a small red dwarf star every three hours and twenty-one minutes, so close that the white dwarf steals its gas. The stolen hydrogen spirals down in a disc and settles on the white dwarf's surface. When the layer reached about twenty million degrees, it ignited in a thermonuclear runaway. In about a day, the star became more than forty million times brighter: the fastest nova ever recorded. Then the white dwarf, still there, began collecting again. This is a classical nova. Astronomers find several of them every year in our galaxy."],
  ["recurrent-nova", "Some novae do not wait thousands of years between eruptions. RS Ophiuchi, about five thousand light-years away in Ophiuchus, has erupted at least nine times since eighteen ninety-eight: in nineteen thirty-three, fifty-eight, sixty-seven, eighty-five, two thousand and six, and most recently in August twenty twenty-one. Here the white dwarf circles a red giant, and drinks from the giant's wind. This white dwarf is very heavy, close to the most a white dwarf can be, so its gravity crushes the stolen gas hard, and only a thin layer is needed to set it off. That is why it erupts every fifteen to twenty years. Each blast slams into the red giant's wind, making shock waves that glow in X-rays, and even accelerate particles to enormous energies. From Earth, RS Ophiuchi jumps from far too faint to see to just visible to the naked eye, for a few days. This is a recurrent nova."],
  ["micronova", "In twenty twenty-two, astronomers using NASA's TESS satellite found something new: tiny novae. On a white dwarf called TV Columbae, about sixteen hundred light-years away, they saw bursts lasting only about ten hours. This white dwarf has a magnetic field strong enough to grab the gas from its partner before it can form a disc, and channel it down onto the two magnetic poles. There, the hydrogen collects in a small patch. When the patch ignites, only that patch burns. Each burst still consumes about twenty million billion kilograms of material, about three and a half billion Great Pyramids of Giza, yet it is only a millionth the size of an ordinary nova. Astronomers called them micronovae."],
  ["symbiotic-nova", "Some novae are in no hurry at all. In nineteen forty-four, a red giant star in the southern constellation of Telescopium, called RR Telescopii, began to brighten. It kept on brightening for four years. And then, instead of fading in a few weeks like most novae, it stayed bright for decades. Eighty years later, it is still fading. RR Telescopii is a symbiotic star: a pulsating red giant, shedding gas in a slow wind, and a white dwarf, far out from it, catching a little of that wind. Because this white dwarf is light, its gas must pile very deep before it ignites, and then it burns steadily, for a lifetime, rather than blowing away in days. It lies about eight thousand eight hundred light-years away. This is a symbiotic nova, the slowest kind."],
  ["type-ia-supernova", "On the eleventh of November fifteen seventy-two, the Danish astronomer Tycho Brahe saw a new star in Cassiopeia, brighter than Jupiter. It stayed visible for sixteen months. Tycho measured it carefully and showed it did not move against the stars, so it was far beyond the Moon, proving that the heavens could change. In two thousand and eight, astronomers caught its light echoing off distant dust, and finally took its spectrum. It was a Type One A supernova. About eight thousand light-years away, a white dwarf had been gaining mass from a companion. As it crept toward one point four times the mass of the Sun, the most a white dwarf can hold up, its carbon ignited everywhere at once. In about a second, the whole star was turned into new elements and flung into space at thousands of kilometres a second. Nothing at all was left behind. Because these explosions are always about equally bright, astronomers use them to measure the universe, and in nineteen ninety-eight they used them to discover that its expansion is speeding up."],
  ["hypernova", "On the twenty-fifth of April nineteen ninety-eight, a satellite caught a gamma-ray burst. In the same spot, astronomers found a supernova, in a spiral galaxy about a hundred and twenty million light-years away. It was the first time a gamma-ray burst was tied to an exploding star. SN nineteen ninety-eight b w was a hypernova. A very massive star, stripped of its outer layers and spinning fast, collapsed straight into a black hole. Gas swirling into the black hole drove two jets out through the star at nearly the speed of light, and the star was blown apart with about ten times the energy of an ordinary supernova. A hypernova is about energy. There are also superluminous supernovae, about brightness, up to a hundred times brighter than usual, which may be powered in other ways. Both are rare: perhaps one in a hundred of the stars that explode."],
  ["luminous-red-nova", "In January two thousand and two, a faint star in Monoceros suddenly brightened. Over the next weeks it flared again and again, to about a million times the brightness of the Sun, and then did something no nova does: it turned deep red, and swelled into one of the largest stars in the galaxy. V eight thirty-eight Monocerotis, about nineteen thousand light-years away, is best explained as two stars that collided and merged. And its flash travelled outward through dust the stars had shed long before, lighting it up shell by shell. For years, the Hubble telescope watched this light echo spread, like ripples on a pond. In two thousand and eight, another star, V thirteen oh nine Scorpii, was caught before, during and after such a merger, proving that it happens. These are luminous red novae: not a flare on a white dwarf, but two ordinary stars becoming one."],
  ["wormhole", "Could there be shortcuts through space? Einstein's theory says that mass curves space. Drawn as a sheet, a heavy object makes a funnel. In nineteen thirty-five, Albert Einstein and Nathan Rosen found that the equations allow two such funnels joined at the throat: a bridge between two distant places, now called a wormhole. From outside, its mouth would look like a sphere, showing the sky at the far end. But such a bridge would pinch shut faster than light could cross it. To hold one open would need exotic matter with negative energy, which ordinary matter does not have, and which may not exist in large amounts. No wormhole has ever been found. It is a beautiful solution of the equations, and no one yet knows whether nature uses it."],
  ["biggest-black-hole", "In the constellation of Canes Venatici is a faint blue dot of light that is not a star. It is TON six eighteen, a quasar, seen as it was ten point eight billion years ago, before the Sun and Earth existed. At its centre is one of the heaviest black holes known, about forty billion times the mass of the Sun. Gas swirling into it is so hot that it outshines its whole galaxy. Its event horizon, the edge from which nothing escapes, would be about eight hundred times the distance from the Earth to the Sun. Our entire planetary system, out to Neptune, is a speck beside it. Even the Voyager spacecraft, after nearly fifty years of travel, would still be deep inside."],
  ["white-hole", "A black hole is a place nothing can leave. Einstein's equations work just as well backwards in time, and run backwards, a black hole becomes a white hole: a place nothing can enter, that only pours out matter and light. Gas falling toward it would be turned back before it arrived. In twenty twelve, two astronomers suggested that a strange gamma-ray burst, GRB oh six oh six one four, seen in June two thousand and six from a galaxy one point six billion light-years away, might have been one. It lasted a hundred and two seconds, but no supernova followed, which bursts that long usually have. Most astronomers think it was something else. No white hole has ever been confirmed, and nothing we know of would make one."],
  ["biggest-star", "How big can a star be? Among the largest known is Stephenson two dash eighteen, a red supergiant about nineteen thousand light-years away, in a star cluster in Scutum, hidden from our eyes by dust. Its width has been estimated at about two thousand one hundred and fifty times the Sun's. Put where the Sun is, its surface would reach out past the orbit of Saturn. Mercury, Venus, Earth, Mars, Jupiter and Saturn would all be inside it. Uranus and Neptune would still be outside, so it would not quite swallow the whole Solar System, and its true size is uncertain; it may be smaller. Stars like this are near the end of their lives, their outer layers ballooned out, so thin that they are emptier than the air around you."],
  ["dyson-sphere", "In nineteen sixty, the physicist Freeman Dyson suggested that a very advanced civilisation might surround its star with a swarm of collectors to catch all of its energy. We could look for them, he said, by the starlight they block, and the heat they give off. In twenty fifteen, volunteers looking through data from the Kepler telescope found a star, about fourteen hundred and seventy light-years away in Cygnus, that dimmed in a way no one could explain: by fifteen percent, then by twenty-two percent, irregularly. It became known as Tabby's star, after the astronomer Tabetha Boyajian. Could it be a Dyson swarm? In twenty eighteen, astronomers found that the dips were deeper in blue light than in red. Solid machines would block every colour equally. Fine dust does not. It was dust. No Dyson sphere has been found, but astronomers still look."],
  ["bootes-void", "Galaxies are not scattered evenly through space. Over billions of years, gravity has gathered them into walls and threads, around enormous empty bubbles, like the foam of a sponge. Beyond the stars of Boötes, about seven hundred million light-years away, is one of the biggest bubbles known: the Boötes Void, about three hundred and thirty million light-years across. Where there would normally be thousands of galaxies, astronomers have found only about sixty. If our Milky Way sat in the middle of it, we might not have known that other galaxies existed until the nineteen sixties."],
  ["pulsar", "In November nineteen sixty-seven, a young astronomer at Cambridge, Jocelyn Bell Burnell, noticed a strange signal on her radio telescope's chart. Pulses, exactly one point three three seven seconds apart, as steady as a clock. Half jokingly, it was labelled LGM one, for little green men. It was the first pulsar, now called PSR B nineteen nineteen plus twenty-one, about two thousand three hundred light-years away. A pulsar is a neutron star, the collapsed core of a dead star, twenty kilometres across, spinning fast. Beams of radio waves pour out above its magnetic poles, and as it spins, they sweep around like a lighthouse. Each time a beam crosses Earth, we hear a pulse. More than three thousand pulsars are now known, and some spin more than seven hundred times a second."],
  ["kepler-186f", "In April twenty fourteen, astronomers announced the first planet found that is about the size of Earth, and at the right distance from its star for liquid water. It is called Kepler one eighty-six f. Its star is a red dwarf, about half the Sun's mass, about five hundred and eighty light-years away in Cygnus. It has five planets. Four orbit very close in, and are too hot. The fifth, f, is just over one Earth wide, and goes round every hundred and thirty days, at the cool edge of the habitable zone, where water could stay liquid. It receives about a third of the sunlight Earth does. Whether it has air, or oceans, or life, no one yet knows. Since then, more such worlds have been found, around Proxima Centauri, and the star TRAPPIST one."],
  ["heart-nebula", "In the constellation of Cassiopeia, long-exposure photographs show a glowing heart. The Heart Nebula is a cloud of hydrogen about two hundred light-years across, seven thousand five hundred light-years away. At its centre is a cluster of young stars, some fifty times the mass of the Sun, whose fierce ultraviolet light makes the gas glow red, and whose winds carve out its shape. But the heart is a matter of where we stand. Fly round it, and the heart disappears. From another star, it would look like something else entirely."],
  ["galaxy-types", "Every faint smudge in a deep photograph may be a whole galaxy. In nineteen twenty-six, Edwin Hubble sorted them by shape. Spirals, like the Whirlpool Galaxy, are flat discs of stars, gas and dust, with arms where new stars are being born. Barred spirals have a bar of stars across the middle, and our own Milky Way is one of them. Ellipticals, like the giant M eighty-seven, are smooth balls of old stars, often made when galaxies merge. Lenticulars are discs that have run out of gas, with no arms. And irregulars, like the Large Magellanic Cloud, have been pulled out of shape. Astronomers now think the observable universe holds about two trillion galaxies."],
  ["j1407b-rings", "In two thousand and seven, a young star in Centaurus called J fourteen oh seven flickered and nearly vanished, over and over, for fifty-six days. Something was passing in front of it. The best explanation is a ring system, round an unseen body, about a hundred and eighty million kilometres across: six hundred and forty times larger than Saturn's rings. Put around Saturn, rings like that would look bigger than the full Moon in our sky. There may be thirty-seven rings, with a gap that a moon could have cleared. The ringed object is called J fourteen oh seven b, and in twenty twenty-four astronomers may have spotted it, drifting free in space."],
  ["horsehead-nebula", "Just below Orion's Belt, beside the star Alnitak, is one of the most famous shapes in the sky: the Horsehead Nebula, about thirteen hundred and seventy-five light-years away. Behind it, a wall of hydrogen glows red, lit by the hot star Sigma Orionis. In front, a pillar of cold dust, about three and a half light-years tall, blocks that glow, and we see its silhouette: a horse's head. It was found in eighteen eighty-eight, on a photographic plate, by Williamina Fleming at Harvard. Ultraviolet light is slowly eating it away; in about five million years, it will be gone. This is the Horsehead, as the Euclid space telescope saw it."],
  ["rogue-planet", "In twenty thirteen, the Pan-STARRS telescope in Hawaii found a faint red object about eighty light-years away that is not a star. PSO J three eighteen point five minus twenty-two is a planet, about eight times the mass of Jupiter, drifting through space all on its own, with no star to circle. It is only about twenty-three million years old, so it still glows with the heat of its birth. It did not come from our Solar System. It was born far from here, either around another star, and flung out by a close pass with another planet, or alone, from a small collapsing cloud of gas, like a star that never grew big enough to shine. There may be billions of planets like it, wandering between the stars."],
  ["eta-carinae", "In March eighteen forty-three, a star in the southern constellation of Carina flared up until it was the second brightest star in the whole sky, after Sirius. Eta Carinae, seven thousand five hundred light-years away, is a pair of enormous stars, the larger about a hundred times the mass of the Sun, near the limit of how heavy a star can be. In its Great Eruption, it threw off about ten Suns' worth of gas, and yet it survived. The gas formed two huge lobes, called the Homunculus, still flying apart today. Dust from the eruption hid the star for a century. Eta Carinae may one day explode for real, as one of the brightest supernovae ever seen from Earth."],
  ["pillars-of-creation", "In nineteen ninety-five, the Hubble Space Telescope photographed three towering columns of gas and dust in the Eagle Nebula, six thousand five hundred light-years away. They became known as the Pillars of Creation. The tallest is about four light-years long. Hot young stars nearby are boiling the cloud away with their ultraviolet light. The pillars are what is left, shielded behind denser knots at their tips. Inside those knots, new stars are forming. In twenty twenty-two, the Webb telescope looked again, seeing through the dust to the newborn stars inside."],
];

export const STORY = Object.fromEntries(CATALOGUE_STORIES);
